[Leonard’s original is also available (same key but capo’d differently).  It’s a little easier to play but Joan’s is more “soaring.]  

FUN FACT:  “Suzanne” is Suzanne Verdal, the girlfriend and later wife of one of Leonard’s friends.  

TIP:  You can play uncapo'd in (G) which will match some of Joan's earlier recordings (but not this one) however you'll have to deal with the (Bm).  I usually don't like to capo this high but this version is the easiest to play and still sounds good up there.}

TIP:  There's a rather complicated looking riff that forms the intro/outro and repeats after the chorus.  It's because Joan plays fingerstyle rather than strums.  You concentrate on the top 4 strings and hold the (C) formation for the entire sequence.  Start with a (C/G) which is just (C) with the bass <g> top string covered by the pinky.  From there you move the pinky off the top string to the fourth string <f> while still holding the (C).  So the pinky goes over the top of the ring finger and then underneath it.  You repeat this once more and for the rest it's just on/off of the pinky on the 4th string.  (Notes may or may note transpose.)


